From dcd6790d8475ea799d7d35246b000e1f8218c99a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:54:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Refuse writes into an incarnation the watch can no longer
 confirm

13.6 completes both descriptors after an UNMAP that failed or timed
out: its unwire_mapping (0x...88ac210) calls unwire_host_mapping, which
returns nothing, and completes the page-table and surface descriptors
whatever became of the request; 14.6.1 and later complete only when it
succeeded. So on 13.6 no UNMAP is no evidence that a mapping's pages are
still wired, and the guest can go on to free the MappingInternal.

Until now an incarnation whose chain stopped reading as the one checked
at MAP was retired from the watch, and a retired mapping is one the
guard lets every write through to; a condemned one lost its
condemnation the same way. Now it is lost instead:

- It is no count and no condemnation: no anomaly is said, nothing is
  withdrawn, its owed frame is not taken, its presents are not refused
  and its pages are still read. It is not sampled any more.
- With the guard on, every host write into it, CPU or GPU, is refused
  (wire_guard_unowned_refused mapping= site= kind= lost= n=), and a
  frame owed to it is dropped when paid (wbdebt_dropped_unowned).
- A fresh MAP of the same MappingInternal ends it (OFF
  wire_guard_unowned_cleared ... reason=fresh_map) and is checked like
  any MAP; so do an UNMAP and another MappingInternal under the id
  (retired internal_reattached). A failed or timed-out UNMAP gives
  nothing back.
- A condemned incarnation that is lost stays condemned, and a MAP
  restating it still does not wash it.

The line is wire_watch identity_lost mapping= internal= reason=<link>
now=[...] checked=[...] condemned=0|1 guard=on|off at= t_us=. The cost
line gains lost= and unowned_refused=, the unmap line lost= and
unowned_refused=.

This holds for every layout. On 14.6.1 and later the guest completes
only after this device answered the UNMAP, so a changed chain with no
UNMAP is as wrong there, and the refusal costs nothing where it never
happens. On 26.6.2 and 27.2 the change is that such a mapping's writes
are refused rather than let through.

Tests (lib, --test-threads=1): 2317 passed, 1 failed: the cursor test,
which reads vendor/qemu/ui/cursor.c, absent from this worktree.
runtime::mapper::wire_watch: 34 passed, 2 of them new. On 13.6 and 27.2
a MappingInternal freed and reused before any drop was seen is lost, not
condemned: its CPU, GPU and debt writes are refused and its pages still
read, until a fresh MAP; with the guard off the watch says so and
refuses nothing. And the whole 13.6 sequence: a timed-out UNMAP, both
counts dropped and condemned, the MappingInternal reused, the
condemnation kept through that and through a restating MAP, then the
late UNMAP and a new MappingInternal written again. With a lost chain
retired as before, both fail (checked). No guest was booted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
